---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Layout title="SEO Case Studies | Waseem's SEO Guide">
  <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <ol>
      <li><a href={`${base}/`}>Home</a></li>
      <li aria-current="page">Case Studies</li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <header>
    <span class="badge">📚 CASE STUDIES</span>
    <h1>Real-World SEO Teardowns</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Public examples that demonstrate the principles from the course. Each one is dissected with concrete changes you can apply tomorrow.</p>
    <div class="reading-time-badge">⏱️ ~15 min per case</div>
  </header>

  <section class="intro">
    <div class="info-box">
      <h3>📖 How to read these case studies</h3>
      <p>Each case follows the same format: <strong>The Setup</strong> (what the page does), <strong>What's Working</strong> (signals worth copying), <strong>What's Missing</strong> (gaps), and <strong>Action Items</strong> (specific changes a developer could ship). The goal is not to criticize — these sites are successful — but to make the SEO principles concrete by examining real pages instead of toy examples.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Case 1 -->
  <article class="case" id="case-1">
    <header class="case-header">
      <span class="case-number">Case 1</span>
      <h2>Title tags in the wild: Nike vs. Adidas product pages</h2>
      <div class="case-meta">📚 Reinforces: Level 1 — Title Tag · 🎯 Topic: e-commerce title-tag patterns</div>
    </header>

    <h3>The setup</h3>
    <p>Both Nike and Adidas sell the same category of running shoes. Both rank top-3 for branded queries and competitively for non-branded ones. Here are their actual title tags from product pages (viewed via "View Page Source" → search for <code>&lt;title</code>):</p>

    <div class="example-block">
      <div class="example-label">Nike (typical men's running shoe page)</div>
      <code class="example-code">Nike Pegasus 41 Men's Road Running Shoes. Nike.com</code>
      <p>59 characters · brand last · functional descriptor + audience + category + brand</p>
    </div>

    <div class="example-block">
      <div class="example-label">Adidas (typical men's running shoe page)</div>
      <code class="example-code">Ultraboost 5 Shoes - Black | adidas US</code>
      <p>38 characters · brand last with pipe separator · product name + descriptor + color + region</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What's working</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Both put the product name first.</strong> When a user searches "Pegasus 41" or "Ultraboost 5," the keyword is at position 1, which Google weights most.</li>
      <li><strong>Brand is last.</strong> "Nike.com" and "adidas US" appear at the end. Users already know the brand from the URL/snippet; using bytes early would be wasted.</li>
      <li><strong>Both stay under 60 characters</strong> — both fit fully in desktop SERPs without truncation.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What's missing / could be better</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Nike's "Nike.com" instead of "| Nike":</strong> The pipe (|) separator is more standard and slightly more readable. Both work, but consistency across the catalog matters more than the choice.</li>
      <li><strong>Adidas could include the audience.</strong> "Ultraboost 5 Men's Shoes - Black | adidas US" would match "men's ultraboost" queries more directly.</li>
      <li><strong>Neither uses model year or version cleverly.</strong> "Pegasus 41 (2024)" or "Ultraboost 5 — New for 2025" can capture "latest" intent queries.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Action items for your own product pages</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Format: <code>[Product name + version] [Audience/variant] [Brand]</code></li>
      <li>Use a separator (<code>|</code>, <code>-</code>, or <code>·</code>) consistently across the catalog.</li>
      <li>Run the title through the <a href={`${base}/labs/title-tag-tester`}>Title Tag Tester lab</a> to check pixel width on mobile (where most retail clicks happen).</li>
      <li>For paginated product variants (color, size), avoid duplicating titles — use canonical tags pointing to the main product.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <!-- Case 2 -->
  <article class="case" id="case-2">
    <header class="case-header">
      <span class="case-number">Case 2</span>
      <h2>Alt text dissection: 4 ways to describe the same image</h2>
      <div class="case-meta">📚 Reinforces: Level 1 — Alt Text · 🎯 Topic: accessibility + image SEO</div>
    </header>

    <h3>The setup</h3>
    <p>You have one photo: a chocolate cake with strawberries on top, photographed for a recipe blog post titled "Easy Chocolate Cake Recipe with Strawberries." Here are four real-world alt-text patterns ranked from worst to best.</p>

    <div class="rank-list">
      <div class="rank rank-bad">
        <div class="rank-tag">❌ Worst</div>
        <div class="rank-content">
          <code>alt=""</code>
          <p>Empty alt is acceptable for purely decorative images, but a feature image like this is decorative <em>and</em> informational. Empty alt here means screen readers skip it AND Google can't index it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rank rank-bad">
        <div class="rank-tag">❌ Bad</div>
        <div class="rank-content">
          <code>alt="image"</code> or <code>alt="cake.jpg"</code>
          <p>Filename or generic "image" is automatically generated by lazy CMS plugins. Provides zero signal. WCAG considers this a failure of Success Criterion 1.1.1.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rank rank-warn">
        <div class="rank-tag">⚠️ OK but stuffed</div>
        <div class="rank-content">
          <code>alt="best chocolate cake recipe easy chocolate cake with strawberries homemade chocolate cake"</code>
          <p>Keyword-stuffed. Google's <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/google-images" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image guidelines</a> explicitly call this out as spam. Screen reader users hear a wall of repetition.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rank rank-good">
        <div class="rank-tag">✅ Best</div>
        <div class="rank-content">
          <code>alt="Chocolate cake topped with fresh strawberries, cut to show fudgy interior"</code>
          <p>Specific, descriptive, naturally contains the topic keywords ("chocolate cake," "strawberries"), useful to a blind user, useful to Google. WCAG-compliant. 78 characters — well within the recommended ~125 character limit before screen readers truncate.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>The rule</h3>
    <p>Ask: <strong>"If the image didn't load, what text would convey the same information?"</strong> That's your alt text. Don't write for SEO — write for the image's purpose. The SEO benefit is a byproduct.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- Case 3 -->
  <article class="case" id="case-3">
    <header class="case-header">
      <span class="case-number">Case 3</span>
      <h2>robots.txt teardown: New York Times</h2>
      <div class="case-meta">📚 Reinforces: Level 2 — robots.txt · 🎯 Topic: enterprise crawl management</div>
    </header>

    <h3>The setup</h3>
    <p>View it yourself: <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/robots.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nytimes.com/robots.txt</a>. The NYT robots.txt is ~5KB long. Here are the most instructive blocks (paraphrased — check the live file for current content):</p>

    <pre class="case-code"><code>User-agent: *
Disallow: /ads/
Disallow: /adx/bin/
Disallow: /aponline/
Disallow: /reuters/
Disallow: /timeswire/
Disallow: /cnet/
Disallow: /college/forums/
Disallow: /pages/college/forums/
Allow: /ads/public/

User-agent: GPTBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: ClaudeBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: PerplexityBot
Disallow: /

Sitemap: https://www.nytimes.com/sitemaps/new/news.xml.gz
Sitemap: https://www.nytimes.com/sitemaps/new/sitemap.xml.gz</code></pre>

    <h3>What's instructive here</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Wire-service syndicated content is blocked.</strong> <code>/aponline/</code>, <code>/reuters/</code>, <code>/cnet/</code> — these are republished from other sources. NYT blocks them from Google to avoid being seen as duplicate content. Smart move.</li>
      <li><strong>Specific ad serving paths are blocked</strong> (<code>/ads/</code>, <code>/adx/bin/</code>) but the public ad assets are explicitly allowed (<code>Allow: /ads/public/</code>). Demonstrates the Allow-overrides-Disallow rule.</li>
      <li><strong>NYT blocks AI training bots</strong> (GPTBot, ClaudeBot, PerplexityBot). This is a deliberate business decision: NYT sued OpenAI in December 2023 for unauthorized training data use. The robots.txt enforces it for compliant bots; the lawsuit handles the rest.</li>
      <li><strong>Multiple sitemaps</strong> are referenced — news sitemap separately from main sitemap (Google has special handling for news sitemaps).</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Test it yourself</h3>
    <p>Paste the NYT robots.txt into the <a href={`${base}/labs/robots-txt-simulator`}>robots.txt Simulator</a>. Try these URLs with <code>User-agent: GPTBot</code>:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>/section/world</code> — blocked (top-level Disallow: /)</li>
      <li><code>/ads/public/logo.png</code> — blocked (GPTBot block has no Allow)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Switch to <code>User-agent: Googlebot</code> and re-test the same URLs — different result. The simulator implements the same parsing rules NYT relies on.</p>

    <h3>Action items for your robots.txt</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Block your duplicate/staging/admin paths explicitly.</li>
      <li>Use one explicit <code>Allow:</code> for any exception you need inside a disallowed parent.</li>
      <li>Reference all sitemap variants (news, image, video) — don't rely on Search Console alone.</li>
      <li>Decide intentionally whether to allow AI training bots. There's no neutral choice — both options have consequences.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <!-- Case 4 -->
  <article class="case" id="case-4">
    <header class="case-header">
      <span class="case-number">Case 4</span>
      <h2>E-E-A-T done right: Healthline medical review pages</h2>
      <div class="case-meta">📚 Reinforces: Level 3 — E-E-A-T Framework · 🎯 Topic: YMYL (Your Money or Your Life) content</div>
    </header>

    <h3>The setup</h3>
    <p>Healthline (healthline.com) dominates Google for "is X safe to eat" / "symptoms of Y" queries — an extremely competitive medical YMYL space. Open any article and you'll see a remarkably consistent pattern that's worth dissecting.</p>

    <h3>What's working (the E-E-A-T checklist they pass)</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Visible author byline with photo + credential</strong> at the top: "Written by [Name], [degree]." Real photo, no stock images.</li>
      <li><strong>Medical reviewer byline:</strong> A second person, separate from the writer, with relevant credentials ("Medically reviewed by [Name], MD") — visible above the fold.</li>
      <li><strong>Published date AND last-updated date</strong> — both visible. The updated date is typically &lt;12 months for important pages.</li>
      <li><strong>Author profile pages exist</strong> and link to LinkedIn, ORCID, or academic affiliation. <code>sameAs</code> is populated in their Person Schema.</li>
      <li><strong>References section at the bottom</strong> — typically 10-30 peer-reviewed citations linking to PubMed, JAMA, etc.</li>
      <li><strong>Visible editorial standards page</strong> in the footer ("Our Editorial Process").</li>
      <li><strong>Contact info, ownership disclosure</strong> in the footer.</li>
      <li><strong>HTTPS, fast page, mobile-optimized</strong> — passes all technical trust signals.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What this teaches non-medical sites</h3>
    <p>You don't need a medical license to copy the format. For any informational content, even on technical topics:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Author + photo + 1-line credential at the top.</li>
      <li>Last-updated date next to publication date.</li>
      <li>References section at the bottom with links to primary sources.</li>
      <li>An editorial process / about page that explains how you research and verify.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This pattern is the literal embodiment of Google's Search Quality Rater Guidelines — applied to your niche, it works the same way.</p>

    <h3>Schema snippet that supports the above</h3>
    <pre class="case-code"><code>&#123;
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "Is Quinoa Safe for Babies?",
  "author": &#123;
    "@type": "Person",
    "name": "Jane Smith, RD",
    "url": "https://www.healthline.com/authors/jane-smith",
    "jobTitle": "Registered Dietitian"
  &#125;,
  "reviewedBy": &#123;
    "@type": "Person",
    "name": "Dr. John Doe, MD",
    "jobTitle": "Pediatrician"
  &#125;,
  "datePublished": "2024-03-15",
  "dateModified": "2026-04-02"
&#125;</code></pre>
  </article>

  <!-- Case 5 -->
  <article class="case" id="case-5">
    <header class="case-header">
      <span class="case-number">Case 5</span>
      <h2>Getting cited in AI Overviews: a paragraph that works</h2>
      <div class="case-meta">📚 Reinforces: Level 3 — AI Overviews & GEO · 🎯 Topic: optimizing paragraphs for LLM citation</div>
    </header>

    <h3>The setup</h3>
    <p>This is a teardown of <em>this very course's</em> Level 2 INP section — designed to be cited by AI Overviews for the query "what is INP core web vital."</p>

    <h3>The paragraph</h3>
    <blockquote class="case-quote">
      <p>"INP (Interaction to Next Paint) is a Core Web Vital that measures how quickly a page visually responds to user interactions. Google replaced FID with INP on March 12, 2024. A good INP score is ≤200ms at the 75th percentile of user interactions; above 500ms is rated 'poor.'"</p>
    </blockquote>

    <h3>Why this paragraph is "citation-shaped"</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>First sentence is the definition.</strong> An LLM can lift it as a complete answer to "what is INP." No need to read further.</li>
      <li><strong>Second sentence anchors the date.</strong> "March 12, 2024" is specific. LLMs prefer precise dates over "in 2024" because they can be verified.</li>
      <li><strong>Third sentence gives the threshold numbers.</strong> "≤200ms" and "above 500ms" are quotable, attribution-friendly facts.</li>
      <li><strong>"75th percentile" adds technical credibility.</strong> Real technical depth signals expertise — fluffy answers say "fast"; expert answers say "75th percentile."</li>
      <li><strong>The H2 directly above the paragraph</strong> matches the likely user question. ("INP: Interaction to Next Paint").</li>
      <li><strong>It's linked to a primary source</strong> (web.dev) elsewhere on the page, satisfying the "outbound citation" signal from the GEO paper.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>How to write your own citation-shaped paragraphs</h3>
    <p>Template:</p>
    <pre class="case-code"><code>[Term] is [definition with concrete attributes].
[Origin / when established, with date].
[Specific metric, threshold, or quantitative fact].</code></pre>

    <p>Apply it to anything: "A canonical tag is..." / "An XML sitemap is..." / "INP is..."</p>

    <div class="info-box compact">
      <p><strong>The 80/20 of GEO writing:</strong> the first paragraph of every section is your "AI candidate." Spend 5× longer on it than the rest. If it would make a good Wikipedia opening sentence, it will make a good AI Overview citation.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="closing">
    <h2>📥 What's next</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Pick one of these patterns and apply it to a real page on your site this week.</li>
      <li>Open the matching <a href={`${base}/labs`}>Lab</a> to test before publishing.</li>
      <li>Submit your audit to your portfolio — these case-study formats double as great portfolio pieces.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <nav class="level-nav">
    <a href={`${base}/labs`} class="nav-btn">🧪 Open the Labs</a>
    <a href={`${base}/sources`} class="nav-btn">📚 Sources →</a>
  </nav>
</Layout>

<style>
  .breadcrumbs { margin-bottom: 30px; }
  .breadcrumbs ol { display: flex; list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; gap: 8px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .breadcrumbs li { color: var(--color-text-muted); }
  .breadcrumbs li:not(:last-child)::after { content: '→'; margin-left: 8px; color: var(--color-text-muted); }
  .breadcrumbs a { color: var(--color-link); text-decoration: none; }

  header { text-align: center; margin-bottom: 40px; }
  .badge {
    display: inline-block;
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #ff7e5f 0%, #feb47b 100%);
    color: white;
    padding: 8px 20px;
    border-radius: 25px;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
  }
  header h1 { font-size: 2.6em; margin-bottom: 10px; color: var(--color-text); }
  .subtitle { font-size: 1.2em; color: var(--color-text-secondary); max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto 20px; line-height: 1.6; }
  .reading-time-badge { display: inline-block; background: var(--color-surface-elevated); padding: 6px 16px; border-radius: 15px; font-size: 0.9em; color: var(--color-text-muted); }

  .info-box {
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #e3f2fd 0%, #bbdefb 100%);
    border-left: 5px solid #2196f3;
    padding: 24px;
    border-radius: 12px;
    margin: 20px 0;
  }
  .info-box.compact { padding: 16px; margin-top: 20px; }
  .info-box h3 { margin: 0 0 10px; color: #0d47a1; }
  .info-box p { margin: 0; color: #0d47a1; line-height: 1.6; }
  :global([data-theme="dark"]) .info-box {
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0d2238 0%, #16314a 100%);
  }
  :global([data-theme="dark"]) .info-box h3,
  :global([data-theme="dark"]) .info-box p { color: #90caf9; }

  .case {
    background: var(--color-surface-elevated);
    border-radius: 20px;
    padding: 40px;
    margin: 30px 0;
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
  }
  .case-header { margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 16px; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--color-border-light); }
  .case-number {
    display: inline-block;
    background: var(--color-brand-gradient);
    color: white;
    padding: 4px 12px;
    border-radius: 12px;
    font-size: 0.85em;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }
  .case h2 { margin: 0; font-size: 1.7em; color: var(--color-text); line-height: 1.3; }
  .case-meta { color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: 0.9em; margin-top: 8px; }

  .case h3 { color: var(--color-brand-primary); margin: 28px 0 12px; font-size: 1.2em; }
  .case p, .case ul, .case ol { color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.75; }
  .case ul, .case ol { padding-left: 24px; }
  .case li { margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .case code { background: var(--color-surface); padding: 2px 7px; border-radius: 4px; color: var(--color-brand-primary); font-size: 0.9em; }

  .example-block {
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-brand-primary);
    padding: 14px 18px;
    border-radius: 8px;
    margin: 12px 0;
  }
  .example-label { font-size: 0.85em; color: var(--color-text-muted); margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600; }
  .example-code {
    display: block;
    font-family: monospace;
    font-size: 1.05em;
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin-bottom: 6px;
    background: transparent;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .example-block p { margin: 0; font-size: 0.85em; color: var(--color-text-muted); }

  .case-code {
    background: #1e1e1e;
    color: #d4d4d4;
    padding: 16px;
    border-radius: 8px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-size: 0.9em;
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin: 16px 0;
  }
  .case-code code { background: transparent; color: #d4d4d4; padding: 0; }

  .case-quote {
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #f3e5f5 0%, #e1bee7 100%);
    border-left: 5px solid #9c27b0;
    padding: 20px;
    border-radius: 12px;
    margin: 20px 0;
  }
  .case-quote p { color: #4a148c; margin: 0; font-style: italic; font-size: 1.05em; }
  :global([data-theme="dark"]) .case-quote {
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #2a1a3a 0%, #3a2a4a 100%);
  }
  :global([data-theme="dark"]) .case-quote p { color: #ce93d8; }

  .rank-list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px; margin: 16px 0; }
  .rank { display: flex; gap: 16px; align-items: stretch; }
  .rank-tag {
    font-size: 0.85em;
    font-weight: 700;
    padding: 12px 14px;
    border-radius: 8px;
    white-space: nowrap;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    color: white;
  }
  .rank-bad .rank-tag { background: var(--color-error); }
  .rank-warn .rank-tag { background: var(--color-warning); color: #856404; }
  .rank-good .rank-tag { background: var(--color-success); }
  .rank-content { background: var(--color-surface); padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; flex: 1; }
  .rank-content code { display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; background: transparent; padding: 0; color: var(--color-text); font-size: 1em; word-break: break-all; }
  .rank-content p { margin: 0; font-size: 0.92em; color: var(--color-text-muted); }

  .closing { margin-top: 30px; }
  .closing h2 { color: var(--color-text); }
  .closing ul { color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.8; padding-left: 24px; }

  .level-nav {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 20px;
    margin-top: 50px;
    padding-top: 30px;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--color-border-light);
  }
  .level-nav .nav-btn {
    padding: 12px 24px;
    background: var(--color-brand-gradient);
    color: white;
    text-decoration: none;
    border-radius: 12px;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    header h1 { font-size: 1.8em; }
    .case { padding: 24px; }
    .case h2 { font-size: 1.4em; }
    .rank { flex-direction: column; }
    .rank-tag { align-self: flex-start; }
    .level-nav { flex-direction: column; }
    .level-nav .nav-btn { width: 100%; text-align: center; }
  }
</style>
